Quiz 2: Pods
    1. What is a pod in Kubernetes?
        a) A single container
        b) A group of one or more containers with shared storage and network
        c) A node in the cluster
        d) A type of deployment

    2. Why are pods considered the smallest deployable units in Kubernetes?
        a) They can run on any node
        b) They contain one or more containers that share resources
        c) They are managed by the control plane
        d) They can be scaled independently

    3. How do pods communicate with each other within a cluster?
        a) Through shared storage
        b) Through the Kubernetes API server
        c) Through the network using IP addresses and ports
        d) Through environment variables

    4. What happens if a pod dies in Kubernetes?
        a) It is permanently removed from the cluster
        b) It triggers its replacement to maintain the desired state
        c) It causes the entire cluster to fail
        d) It is automatically restarted without replacement

    5. Which component of Kubernetes is responsible for scheduling pods onto nodes?
        a) API server
        b) Controller Manager
        c) Scheduler
        d) Cloud Controller
      
    6. A Pod can have only one container in it. True or False?
        a) True
        b) False

    7. What is the right approach to scale an application in Kubernetes?
        a) Scale the number of nodes in the cluster
        b) Scale the number of pods using a deployment
        c) Scale the number of containers within a pod
        d) Scale the resources allocated to a single pod



      Answers:
        1. b) A group of one or more containers with shared storage and network
        2. b) They contain one or more containers that share resources  
        3. c) Through the network using IP addresses and ports
        4. b) It triggers its replacement to maintain the desired state
        5. c) Scheduler 
        6. b) False (A Pod can have multiple containers that share the same network and storage resources)
        7. b) Scale the number of pods using a deployment (Scaling is typically done by increasing the number of pods managed by a deployment, which allows for better load distribution and fault tolerance.)